{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '355,371p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '1680,1705p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '1810,1850p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '2770,2794p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '1680,1735p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '2860,2890p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '35,50p' docs/agents/known-gaps.md; head -38 test/yin/vm/ucf/ledger_fixtures.cljc; rg -n 'bytes->|hex->|segment-address|path' test/yin/vm/ucf/ledger_fixtures.cljc | tail -10\"","aggregated_output":"- **Module stores travel by reachability.** A `:module-store` row is\n  emitted for every `store-of` an `E` or closure names, transitively\n  through the values of stores already discovered (`module-store-nodes`);\n  a named store the kernel lacks is `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :missing-module-store`.\n- **Linked registry entries do not travel.** The row table has no slot\n  for the receiver's module registry; only module stores do. A name a\n  carried closure resolves through the source's registry is a\n  **composition prerequisite** of the receiver: row 17's receiver is\n  given the source's registry value, and without it the relift refuses\n  `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:obligations #{host.mod/f}}`. The receiver's\n  registry is kept as given; there is no isolated-receiver pruning.\n- **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: phase 7 admits exclusive custody on rows.\n  Fork custody is nil on every task row. An exclusive blocked or parked\n  root carries policy, occurrence, arbitration and next-op-seq, with\n  origin on a successor; a halted exclusive root carries origin only.\n  Every child has nil custody and is checked in the root's context.\n   plus, for every reachable `:closure` instruction, that closure's body\n   from its entry (reachable code can still construct it). Every free\n   occurrence (`scan/free-occurrences`) at a live pc must be **answered\n   in that context**, in `resolve-var`'s order: a reserved name; the\n   context's own `E`; the **one** store `engine/active-store` selects for\n   that `E` (the root store when `E` has no `store-of`, else the module\n   store it names, both of which travel); a profiled primitive (a\n   `:host`-class profile refuses `:yin.k/non-portable\n   :host-state-primitive` at that point, so a host primitive shadowed by\n   `E` or the store lifts); an installing module's export (`m/x` built\n   with `(symbol (name m) (name x))`); a module the registry resolves for\n   a namespaced name. Any unanswered name is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`,\n   `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`, `:yin.k/missing {:obligations #{…}}`.\n   *Why per context:* codex F3, the union of all module-store keys\n   answered names the running closure could not resolve. *Why live\n   code:* codex F6, a numeric or whole-body scan refused valid\n   continuations whose unreachable branches named a missing symbol.\n   The parser admits no backward jump (§3.1 of the frozen design), so no\n   carried vector exercises the backward edge; `live-pcs` is public and\n   is tested directly on a hand-built vector with a `:jump` to an earlier\n   pc. Discharge runs at lift only; the lower does not re-discharge free\n   names against the receiver (§7.2).\n7. **Trees** (§6.1): every carried segment has a non-empty entry in the\n   `:source-index` option, else `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with `:yin.k/trees`.\n8. **The task.** `requires` is `{:yin.k/cursor-profiles … :yin.k/segments\n   … :yin.k/trees …}`; the abstract task is initially assembled with nil custody.\n| custody | `check-custody!` | `custody-baseline` over the tree; fork refused under `:exclusive`; halted exclusive needs no grant; blocked/parked exclusive validates grant evidence, pack checkpoint, binding, lease/holder, arbitration, epoch, live tenure and input prefix, then protection in both directions and op-id presence against enrollment; `:fenced` skips grant/protection | grammar's `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds; `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, `:yin.k/awaiting-grant`, `:yin.k/not-holder`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied` |\n\n**Amended 2026-10-16 (module-pins, R4).** After custody validation and\nbefore attachment, every carried install child must have its manifest\naddress listed under its module name in the receiving composition's\n`opts {:yin.k/admitted-modules {name manifest-address}}`. The default is empty:\nan absent or different address refuses as\n`{:yin.k/status :yin.k/unsatisfied\n  :yin.k/missing {:yin.k/modules {name carried-address}}}`.\nThis applies to children throughout the task tree, including fenced\nrestoration; `holder.export/rehydrate-fenced` forwards the composition's\n`:yin.k/admitted-modules` option to the lower. The lift never carries the sender's\n`:yin.admit/id`, `:yin.admit/decision`,\n`:yin.admit/asserters` and `:yin.admit/address` from an install\nresponse; a carried admission field is refused as\n`:yin.k/undecodable :install-response` before `link-install` can record it as receiver evidence. The\ncomposition supplies admissions from its own policy, never from the pack.\n\nThen **attachment** (step 11, `attach-streams!`): one `attach!` per\ndistinct stream identity of the whole tree, `:yin.k/unsatisfied\n{:dao.stream/identity …}` when the composition declines. Then **restore**\n(step 12, `restore-task`): the carried vectors attached by A into a\nreceiver emptied of code, so frame and closure addresses map through the\nreceiver's own aliases and no source-local segment number travels; fresh\nresource keys per stream identity and per cursor cell; values re-sealed\nunder the receiver's owner; waits in `lower-frame!`'s entry shapes, a\n`tail` with `{:deliver :return}` and no window, env or pc; parked records\nwith a cycle guard; the store through `engine/store-put`; module stores;\ninstall children spawned by the engine's spawn discipline and then\nrestored recursively (the spawn's initial control is replaced, no\n`vm/run`); the free environment as both `:env` and `:free-env`; the id\ncounter `max(local, carried)` for ordinary resume. Restore meets no carrier\ndefect, by\nconstruction of the stages above. The lower does **not** re-discharge\nfree names against the receiver: a name a closure resolves through the\nregistry is the composition prerequisite of §2.5, and the receiver's\nregistry is kept as given. The kinds `:role :custody :incomplete-install\n:kind :parked-id :requires :segment :install-phase :install-response\n:install-child :closure-origin :closure-binders` and the `:yin.k/stores`\ncensus are the engineer's and the fix rounds' naming; the earlier text\nnamed none of them (deviation 9, accepted with the requirement that every\n  refuse any id. Lower checks protection/enrollment in both directions\n  before attachment. The broader cursor-cell stream, envelope-id and module\n  correlation agreement promised by draft §8 is not implemented as a\n  general check; Q3's install-parent agreement is implemented.\n- **Q5, answered C by the Architect threat model (2026-10-16);\n  host-export carrier ruled in Architect Part B, implementation pending.** The ruling\n  requires `requires {:yin.k/modules {name manifest-address-or-nil}}`,\n  equal-address receiver re-discharge before attach, and equal export\n  profile addresses for used exports of host-module nil pins through\n  `:yin.k/host-exports {qualified-symbol profile-keyword}` in `requires`.\n  The r2c decision resolves empties: both maps are always emitted, `{}`\n  when empty; missing keys refuse `:yin.k/undecodable :requires`.\n  Implementation is pending resolution of Part B.1.3's string address values\n  versus the registry's Jing keyword addresses (I-8). Until it lands, the implementation still\n  has the following gap: free names are discharged at lift\n  only (§7.1); the lower checks carried segments, trees, store census,\n  closures and primitives, and a name a closure resolves through the\n  registry is a composition prerequisite (§2.5). A receiver lacking that\n  registry entry accepts the carrier and fails at run time\n  (`Unable to resolve symbol`). This behavior is not the accepted final\n  contract; the receiver-side pin check remains required.\n- **Q6, conservatism of the live-code rule.** `live-pcs` follows both\n  successor edges of every branch and enters every reachable closure\n  body, with no constant propagation. The harness's `(if false (fn []\n  ghost) (next-of c))` case transports only because the lift happens at\n  …}` carries the counter verbatim; the lowered machine adopts\n  `max(local, carried)` so generated ids never repeat, and ids already\n  minted (gensym results in env, stack, or store) travel as ordinary values,\n  unaffected by the counter.\n- **Referenced parked records travel.** The fixed point\n  of §7.6.1 collects every `:resume` operand in reachable code and every\n  `[:parked id]` value from reachable environments. Each must resolve\n  within this task to a parked record carried under\n  `:yin.k/scheduler :yin.k/parked {pid → frame}`. A missing id referenced\n  as a code operand is reported as an unsupplied requirement\n  (`:yin.k/unsatisfied`). A missing id referenced by an active value\n  refuses the lift (`:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:foreign-parked-ref`),\n  as it represents corrupted state. Parked records not named by the\n  reachable graph do not travel.\n\n### 7.6.4 Modules\n\n`:yin.k/modules` names each module by the content address of its published\nmanifest, on the model of `dao.space.index/publish-index!` (`dao.jing.md`,\n*Publication from an agent*). A module is satisfied when the resumer holds a\nmodule registry entry (`yin.vm.module` — a value, supplied by the\ncomposition) whose manifest address is equal; it is not satisfied by a\nsame-named module of different content. Module *code* is segments and is\ncovered by `:yin.k/segments` and the manifest's own addressing; the manifest\npins the effect handlers, the exported primitive profiles (§7.5.2), and —\nper §7.6.1 — the store footprint the fixed point needs to claim\n`:complete`.\n\n### 7.6.5 The satisfaction check\n\nBefore lowering, the resumer evaluates `:yin.k/requires` against what it\nholds and answers with one outcome:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/status    :yin.k/unsatisfied\n :yin.k/discovery :incomplete                    ; or :blocked, §7.6.1 — the computed status of the closure itself\n :yin.k/missing   {:yin.k/segments       #{:segment/blake3-9c02…}\n                   :yin.k/primitives     {println <expected profile>}\n                   :yin.k/modules        {my.mod …}\n                   :yin.k/streams        #{<identity>}\n                   :yin.k/cursor-profiles #{:dao.stream/file-v1}}}  ; keys present only where something is missing\n```\n\nThe check reports everything missing in one pass *that the fixed point could\nsee*, and says which kind of pass it was: `:complete` (the closure is fully\ncomputed; the missing list is exhaustive), `:incomplete` (a module footprint\nwas undeclared; the missing list is a lower bound), or `:blocked` (a missing\nsegment stopped discovery; satisfying `:yin.k/segments` and re-running the\ncheck is the only way forward). A missing segment may be resolved by\n`dao.jing` fetch (§7.3.4); a missing primitive, module, or cursor profile\ncannot be resolved by the resumer and is a routing decision for whoever\nchose this resumer. A resumer never lowers a partially satisfied value; a\nhost with no implementation for something answers with the qualified\nunsupported outcome, which `datom.world.md` calls a correct outcome, not a\ngap to be filled.\n\n\n- **Authority grounding and wiring.** The arbitration `dao.space`, its\n  transactor as grantor, the occurrence ledger with epochs, and the\n  attribution resolver `dao.lease.md` requires, are composition duties; the\n  first composition to ship UCF is `yin.repl.core`'s handoff demo, and\n  its wiring becomes the worked example. A composition without a\n  transactable space offers `:yin.k/fork` only.\n- **Receiver module satisfaction and install admission (2026-10-16).**\n  Architect verdict C requires registry bindings pinned by manifest address\n  (§7.6.4/§7.6.5) and receiver re-discharge before attachment. The module-pin\n  carrier uses `:yin.k/host-exports` in `requires` for used host export profile\n  addresses (Architect Part B, ruled 2026-10-16). Implementation is pending:\n  r2c resolves empty emission (both maps always present, `{}` when empty),\n  but stops on string-or-nil carrier values versus actual Jing keyword\n  registry addresses (I-8). Name presence\n  does not establish equal-address satisfaction.\n  Carried install admission (R4) is implemented by the lower's explicit\n  `:yin.k/admitted-modules {name manifest-address}` option, default empty, and\n  the lift never carries sender admission evidence; a carried admission field\n  is refused as `:yin.k/undecodable :install-response`. The fetch loop remains\n  composition work: fetch before the grant proposal (14.2.3 step 2), retry\n  the same pack bytes, at most one require per missing module and two\n  lowers per attempt, under a per-run lease. The loop is **forbidden** to\n  seed `:name-env` from the carrier, switch derivation to `:trusted`, pass\n  carrier-named `:fallback` formats, or require names outside the reported\n  `:yin.k/modules`. No fetch, clock, lease or admission policy belongs in\n  the VM, `resolve-var` or the pure lower.\n- **Execution identity.** Occurrence minting, the `:yin.k/origin` chain,\n  retry idempotence (one occurrence per park), and successor completion as\n  a grantor ledger transition need conformance tests, including the crash\n  windows of §7.7.5.\n| `(untrust <own>)` says \"always trusted\" on an `:external` session, where the external admitter decides | fable AL-4-nits review P3-2 | yin.repl | next yin.repl slice | P3 |\n| Closed 2026-10-16: `:try?` carried by all three UCF link pending variants; lifted try waiters retain their return contract | G1 / L-d-1b slice: row/frame and remote lift/lower; portable refusal, install and malformed-boolean tests in `semantic/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`, frame bijection in `ucf/rows_test.cljc` | UCF | L-d-1b closed | P1 |\n| try joiner's `:link-id` is the install's parent id; drop `:link-id` from install waiters, loss datum carries `:module` | fable L-d-1 review P3-1 | linker | L-d-1b | P3 |\n| `publish/requirements` pins only operator `require`; module-level `try-require` yields no pin | fable L-d-1 review P3-3 | linker | closed in L-d-3 | P3 |\n| publication-time-unavailable optional imports cannot be represented without concrete segment pins | mob consensus I4 Q1 | yang.python / linker | open | P3 |\n| C4 scanner pins `[:c4 :py]` / `[:c4 :t]` in `test/yin/vm/semantic/scanner_goldens.cljc` stale since I4-a; slow `scan-test` bodies fail on master until regenerated | follow-up-1 / owner scheduling | yang.python seat / muse | open: regenerate once at the end of I5/I6 with Architect review | P2 |\n| lower `keyed` builder (`ucf/rows.cljc`, ~:2135-2137: `:bindings`, `:store`, `:window` and cursor-cell census rows) has no `built`-style host-collapse guard for identifier keys: on JS two slash-crossed symbols (`(symbol \"a/b\" \"c\")` vs `(symbol \"a\" \"b/c\")`) as binding names merge in the host map; hand-crafted carrier only (the reader cannot produce such symbols and `:slot-kind` does not split on `/`); decode already refuses the numeric `equiv` collapses | follow-up-1 round-2 Architect confirm P3 | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | open: mirror the `built` count guard in `keyed`, one test on Node (JS-only case) | P3 |\n| Q5: receiver accepts registry dependencies by name only; Architect verdict C requires module identity pins and receiver re-discharge | fetch threat model §4.2, §6; Architect module-pins Part B | VM Runtime / Architect | host-export carrier ruled 2026-10-16: `:yin.k/host-exports` in `requires`; r2c resolves empties (both maps always present), but implementation stops on string-or-nil carrier values versus actual Jing keyword registry addresses (I-8); module pins not implemented | P1 |\n| R4: carried install child bypasses receiver admission and carries sender evidence | fetch threat model §4.4 | VM Runtime | landed as module-pins commit 1 (origin/master ce76c846): explicit `:yin.k/admitted-modules` name/address gate before attach; lift never carries sender admission fields; a carried admission field refuses `:yin.k/undecodable :install-response`; the pin carrier is module-pins commit 2 | P1 |\n| Composition fetch loop per-run lease and budget absent | fetch threat model §4.3; reserved `:lease` policy | composition | outside module-pins slice; no fetch loop added | P2 |\n| Holder driver passes no admitted-modules map; install-child handoff through the driver refuses | Architect module-pins review P2-1 | VM Runtime | implemented in module-pins r2b (uncommitted): composition config retained and forwarded to lowering and fenced recovery; parked-installer driver regression covers absent and admitted config | P2 |\n(ns yin.vm.ucf.ledger-fixtures\n  \"The cross-host ledger fixture of M-next C slice C12: one arbitration\n   ledger holding every fact kind of yin.vm.ucf.ledger, built by a\n   portable script (`build`) and pinned as its journal frames.\n\n   The pins live in test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/ledger-v2.txt: a first\n   line `ledger-v2`, a second `projection <digest>`, the BLAKE3 hex of\n   the canonical bytes of the projection the frames fold to, then one\n   journal frame per line as lowercase hex, header first.  Regenerate\n   the file from a one-off JVM script with the string `render` returns,\n   saved to `path`; the test proves on every host that the file equals\n   `render`, so every host's rebuild has the pinned bytes.\n\n   Version 2 (phase 7a): the offered and reported checkpoints are\n   version-3 row packs (yin.vm.ucf.rows-checkpoint-fixtures), each stored\n   and recorded under its pack address, its root id inside the baseline.\n\n   Nothing here writes a file.\"\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            [dao.jing.cbor-fixtures :as cfx]\n            [dao.jing.mem :as mem]\n            [dao.lease :as lease]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [dao.stream.journal :as journal]\n            [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ringbuffer]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.authority :as authority]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.authority.admission :as admission]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.authority.completion :as completion]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.authority.grant :as grant]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.authority.input :as input]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows-checkpoint-fixtures :as fx]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.custody :as custody]))\n\n\n(def path\n  \"The fixture file, relative to the repository root.\"\n11:   saved to `path`; the test proves on every host that the file equals\n37:(def path\n106:    (grant/offer! a store (fx/segment-address bs) bs \"carrier\")))\n133:                        (completion/resumed o l (fx/segment-address bs))\n241:  (cfx/bytes->hex bs))\n257:                                     (str/split-lines (cfx/read-path path)))]\n259:      (throw (ex-info \"not a ledger-v2 fixture\" {:path path})))\n261:     :frames (mapv cfx/hex->bytes frames)}))\n267:  (cfx/read-path path))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
